# 7. CLI
# ==============================================================================

print_usage() {
    cat <<'EOF'
yt-resolve — the YouTube resolve engine: a handle becomes bytes (non-interactive)

Usage:
  yt-resolve [-f MODE] [-S SORT] [--quality TIER] [-j|-J] -- <video-id | URL>
  yt-resolve --info [-j|-J] -- <video-id | URL>
  yt-resolve --transcript [--sub-lang L] [-j|-J] -- <video-id | URL>
  yt-resolve --items [--cursor TOKEN] [-j|-J] -- <list-id | playlist URL | channel>
  yt-resolve --auth [-j]
  yt-resolve --capabilities [-j]

The handle is either a YouTube URL or an 11-character video id (what `yt-search -j`
returns as results[].id). Knowing that shape is this engine's job, not the player's.

Options:
  -f MODE  Playback mode the format is chosen for: audio | video | fast | ascii | viz
           (default: audio). The MODE -> yt-dlp format table lives here, not in t-play.
  -S SORT  yt-dlp format-sort override (--format-sort)
  --quality TIER  Quality tier: auto | low | medium | high (default: auto). The
           (mode, tier) -> format-sort table lives here, not in t-play; -S overrides it
  -j       JSON envelope (see below)
  -J       Full raw yt-dlp record
  --info   Metadata only (title, channel, chapters, description) — nothing is resolved
  --transcript  Caption track as clean text, ready to drop into a prompt
  --sub-lang L  Caption language(s): a comma-separated PRIORITY CHAIN
                (default: en,zh-Hans,zh,ja)
  --items  List the videos of a playlist (PL…), an album (OLAK5uy_…), a channel's uploads
           (UU…) or a channel (…/@handle, …/channel/UC…) — one yt-dlp call. Takes the id,
           the URL, or the list= of a watch URL; a channel is read at its videos tab. A mix
           (RD…) is refused: it is regenerated per request, so its pages would not line up.
           Every element is an item record, so the list pipes into t-playlist --add and
           t-play --queue unmapped. At most 500 videos per call — has_more/next_cursor say
           whether there are more and how to ask
  --cursor TOKEN  Continue an --items listing: pass back the next_cursor of the envelope
           before it. Opaque, and the same shape in every engine of this suite
  --auth   Report the cookie decision: which browser profile playback will read, and
           whether it exists here. No network, no handle. Says cookies are SENT, not
           that they are accepted — an expired login still reports "cookie"
  --capabilities  The flags this half accepts, less the -l --color -h -V every command
           has. No network, no handle, no dependency: how a caller learns what to ask
  --color MODE  auto | always | never (default: auto)
  -h       This help
  -V       Print the suite version

Resolve JSON (-j): {"status","engine","id","url","title","duration","mode","format",
                    "selected","selected_resolution",
                    "stream_urls":[...],"http_headers":{...},"retried"}
  format is the selection string SENT; selected is the format yt-dlp actually PICKED, and
  selected_resolution its resolution ("audio only" when there is no video).
  stream_urls is VIDEO FIRST: element 0 is what a player opens, element 1 (present only
  when the format merged two streams) is its separate audio track.
  http_headers is a REQUIRED key (possibly {}) — the headers the URLs must be fetched with.

Info JSON (--info -j): {status,engine,id,title,url,channel,uploader,upload_date,duration,
                        duration_fmt,view_count,like_count,live_status,description,chapters}
Transcript JSON (--transcript -j): {status,engine,id,url,lang,is_auto,chars,segment_count,text}
Items JSON (--items -j): {status,engine,id,url,title,count,total,has_more,next_cursor,
                          items:[{n,engine,id,url,title,duration,duration_fmt}]}
  count is what came back; total is what the site says the list holds. They differ when the
  list is longer than the cap or when an entry was private, deleted or otherwise unplayable,
  and the envelope does not say which — the fix for either is the same.
  has_more says whether the CONTAINER holds rows beyond this batch (never whether rows were
  filtered out of it), and next_cursor is the token to ask for them with — null exactly when
  has_more is false. total is null when the site has not said the count yet: on a channel it
  arrives with the batch that reaches the end. n numbers the rows of THIS batch, from 1.
  -J returns the raw --flat-playlist record, entries unfiltered — a private or deleted one is
  dropped from -j's items and is still in there.
  A `list=` is expanded ONLY by this verb. Every other verb here still passes
  --no-playlist, so a single video's URL never turns into a queue on its own.

Auth JSON (--auth -j): {status,engine,auth,cookie_browser,profile_found,cookie_readable}
  auth is "cookie" exactly when cookie_browser is not "none" AND profile_found.
  cookie_readable: can this process read the store (true|false; null = not tried/no probe).
Capabilities JSON (--capabilities -j): {status,engine,flags[]}

Exit codes: 0 ok · 1 usage/validation · 2+ a missing dependency, or a propagated
            yt-dlp failure (see .reason)

Environment:
  Defaults for every knob below live in <checkout>/config, declared once for the whole
  suite. Override per user in ${XDG_CONFIG_HOME:-~/.config}/ting/config (KEY=value, one
  per line, # comments; the pre-rename .../uting/config is read when there is no ting
  one); TING_CONFIG relocates it, and UT_CONFIG still does. Chain: flag > environment > your
  config > the shipped defaults. See ARCH-cli-contract.md「配置面」.
  YT_COOKIE_BROWSER      Browser profile cookies are read from (default: chrome; "none"
                         for anonymous) — the decision --auth reports
  YT_AUDIO_FORMAT        Format for audio/viz modes (default: ba/b)
  YT_VIDEO_FORMAT        Format for video/ascii modes (default: bv*+ba/b)
  YT_VIDEO_FORMAT_FAST   Format for fast mode
  YT_SUB_LANG_CHAIN      --transcript language priority chain (default: en,zh-Hans,zh,ja)

To search: yt-search <query>   ·   To play: t-play <id|URL>
EOF
}

# Every flag this half takes beyond the -l --color -h -V every command has, stated ONCE: the
# unknown-flag refusal prints it for a person and --capabilities answers with it for a caller,
# so the two cannot disagree about what this engine has (ARCH-cli-contract.md「命令规格」).
RESOLVE_FLAGS=(-f -S --quality -j -J --info --transcript --sub-lang --items --cursor --auth --capabilities)

has_format=0
positional=()
while (($#)); do
    case "$1" in
    -h | --help)
        print_usage
        exit 0
        ;;
    -V | --version)
        # Answered before ANY dependency gate: a version is the first thing a bug report
        # needs, and needing yt-dlp installed to find out which version you have is
        # backwards.
        printf 'yt-resolve %s\n' "$UT_VERSION"
        exit 0
        ;;
    -j | --json)
        has_format=1
        OUTPUT_MODE="json"
        shift
        ;;
    -J | --json-full)
        has_format=1
        OUTPUT_MODE="json_full"
        shift
        ;;
    -l | --list)
        has_format=1
        OUTPUT_MODE="list"
        shift
        ;;
    --info)
        set_action info --info
        shift
        ;;
    --items)
        set_action items --items
        shift
        ;;
    --cursor=*)
        CURSOR="${1#*=}"
        shift
        ;;
    --cursor)
        [[ $# -ge 2 ]] || { echo "yt-resolve: --cursor requires a value" >&2; exit 1; }
        CURSOR="$2"
        shift 2
        ;;
    --auth)
        set_action auth --auth
        shift
        ;;
    --capabilities)
        set_action capabilities --capabilities
        shift
        ;;
    --transcript | --subtitles)
        set_action transcript "$1"
        shift
        ;;
    --sub-lang=* | --sub-langs=*)
        SUB_LANG="${1#*=}"
        shift
        ;;
    --sub-lang | --sub-langs)
        [[ $# -ge 2 ]] || { echo "yt-resolve: $1 requires a value" >&2; exit 1; }
        SUB_LANG="$2"
        shift 2
        ;;
    --color=*)
        COLOR_MODE="${1#*=}"
        shift
        ;;
    --color)
        [[ $# -ge 2 ]] || { echo "yt-resolve: $1 requires a value" >&2; exit 1; }
        COLOR_MODE="$2"
        shift 2
        ;;
    -f | --format-mode)
        [[ $# -ge 2 ]] || { echo "yt-resolve: $1 requires a value" >&2; exit 1; }
        FORMAT_FLAG="$1"
        # Canonical modes only — the alias table (ba/bv/…) is t-play's, which normalizes
        # before it calls an engine (ARCH-cli-contract.md「命令规格」).
        validate_enum "$2" audio video fast ascii viz ||
            die "-f must be one of: audio, video, fast, ascii, viz"
        PLAYBACK_MODE="$2"
        shift 2
        ;;
    -S)
        [[ $# -ge 2 ]] || { echo "yt-resolve: $1 requires a value" >&2; exit 1; }
        FORMAT_FLAG="$1"
        FORMAT_SORT="$2"
        shift 2
        ;;
    --quality)
        [[ $# -ge 2 ]] || { echo "yt-resolve: $1 requires a value" >&2; exit 1; }
        FORMAT_FLAG="$1"
        # Canonical tiers only — the alias table, if one ever grows one, is t-play's
        # (the same rule as -f: the engine seam carries canonical spellings).
        validate_enum "$2" auto low medium high ||
            die "--quality must be one of: auto, low, medium, high"
        QUALITY="$2"
        shift 2
        ;;
    # The gate. Resolution neither plays nor tracks a player, and it does not search:
    # a caller holding the wrong verb is told which one it wants (ARCH-cli-contract.md「门模型」).
    -d | --detach | --status | --stop | --set-volume | --id | --all | --volume)
        echo "yt-resolve: $1 is a playback flag — use t-play for playback" >&2
        exit 1
        ;;
    -n | -m | -M | -s)
        echo "yt-resolve: $1 is a search flag — use yt-search for searching" >&2
        exit 1
        ;;
    --)
        shift
        # `--` stops FLAG parsing, not argument validation: a handle beginning with '-'
        # must survive, and a second handle is still an error. ARCHITECTURE.md「端到端控制流」.
        while (($#)); do
            positional+=("$1")
            shift
        done
        break
        ;;
    -*)
        echo "yt-resolve: unknown flag '$1' (resolve flags: ${RESOLVE_FLAGS[*]})" >&2
        exit 1
        ;;
    *)
        positional+=("$1")
        shift
        ;;
    esac
done

# --auth asks about the ENGINE, not about a handle, so it is the one verb here that takes
# no positional — and a handle passed with it is rejected rather than ignored, the same way
# -f next to --info is (ARCH-cli-contract.md「门模型」).
TARGET=""
if [[ "$ACTION" == "auth" ]]; then
    if ((${#positional[@]} > 0)); then
        echo "yt-resolve: --auth takes no argument — it reports this engine's cookie decision, not a handle's" >&2
        exit 1
    fi
elif [[ "$ACTION" == "capabilities" ]]; then
    if ((${#positional[@]} > 0)); then
        echo "yt-resolve: --capabilities takes no argument — it reports what this engine accepts, not anything about a handle" >&2
        exit 1
    fi
else
    # --items wants a CONTAINER, so it says so: a caller handed the "video id" sentence while
    # asking for a playlist would go looking for the wrong handle.
    if ((${#positional[@]} == 0)); then
        if [[ "$ACTION" == "items" ]]; then
            echo "yt-resolve: a list id or a playlist URL is required (see 'yt-resolve -h')" >&2
        else
            echo "yt-resolve: a video id or URL is required (see 'yt-resolve -h')" >&2
        fi
        exit 1
    fi
    if ((${#positional[@]} > 1)); then
        if [[ "$ACTION" == "items" ]]; then
            echo "yt-resolve: takes exactly one list id or playlist URL (got ${#positional[@]})" >&2
        else
            echo "yt-resolve: takes exactly one video id or URL (got ${#positional[@]})" >&2
        fi
        exit 1
    fi
    TARGET="${positional[0]}"
fi

# The default output is prose; -j/-J/-l override.
((has_format)) || OUTPUT_MODE="list"

if ! validate_enum "$COLOR_MODE" auto always never; then
    die "--color must be one of: auto, always, never"
fi
# Validate the language list HERE rather than inside the fetch so a typo costs no network
# round trip; the value reaches yt-dlp as one argv element, so this is a contract check,
# not a quoting one.
if [[ -n "$SUB_LANG" ]]; then
    [[ "$ACTION" == "transcript" ]] || die "--sub-lang applies only to --transcript"
    [[ "$SUB_LANG" =~ ^[A-Za-z0-9][A-Za-z0-9_,.-]*$ ]] ||
        die "--sub-lang takes a comma-separated list of language tags (e.g. en or zh-Hans,en)"
fi
# THE CURSOR IS A TOKEN THIS ENGINE MINTED, so both ways of getting it wrong are answered here,
# before a packet is spent: it belongs to --items (no other verb has batches), and it has one
# shape. The shape is the same in all three engines — `o:` and an item offset — so a cursor
# handed to the wrong engine is caught in argv instead of a request later
# (ARCH-engine.md「容器（--items）」).
if [[ -n "$CURSOR" ]]; then
    [[ "$ACTION" == "items" ]] || die "--cursor continues an --items listing — pass --items"
    [[ "$CURSOR" =~ ^o:[0-9]+$ ]] ||
        die "'$CURSOR' is not a cursor — pass back the next_cursor an --items envelope handed you"
    CURSOR_OFFSET="${CURSOR#o:}"
fi

# The mirror of that check, and the other half of the gate t-play used to hold for
# --transcript: -f / -S pick a FORMAT, and only stream resolution resolves one. Next to
# --info or --transcript they were accepted and quietly did nothing, which is precisely
# what a gating layer exists to reject rather than ignore (ARCH-cli-contract.md「门模型」).
if [[ -n "$FORMAT_FLAG" && "$ACTION" != "stream" ]]; then
    die "$FORMAT_FLAG selects a stream format — it does not apply to --$ACTION"
fi

# -J is the RAW yt-dlp record, and neither --auth nor --capabilities runs yt-dlp: there is
# no raw record for it to return. Rejected rather than silently downgraded to -j.
if [[ ( "$ACTION" == "auth" || "$ACTION" == "capabilities" ) && "$OUTPUT_MODE" == "json_full" ]]; then
    die "-J returns the raw yt-dlp record — --$ACTION runs no yt-dlp; use -j"
fi

# --capabilities answers ahead of every gate that needs a tool, jq included: it is how a caller
# learns what to ask this engine, so it must answer on a machine that cannot run any of it yet.
# The flags are fixed ASCII words, so the envelope is printed rather than built.
if [[ "$ACTION" == "capabilities" ]]; then
    if [[ "$OUTPUT_MODE" == "json" ]]; then
        caps=""
        for f in "${RESOLVE_FLAGS[@]}"; do caps+="${caps:+,}\"$f\""; done
        printf '{"status":"ok","engine":"%s","flags":[%s]}\n' "$ENGINE_NAME" "$caps"
    else
        printf '%s\n' "${RESOLVE_FLAGS[*]}"
    fi
    exit 0
fi

# --auth answers ahead of the dependency gate and the handle grammar, the way -V does: it
# reports configuration, so it must not need the tool it is reporting the configuration of.
if [[ "$ACTION" == "auth" ]]; then
    [[ "$OUTPUT_MODE" == "json" ]] && require_deps jq
    resolve_auth
    exit 0
fi

# --items has its own handle grammar: a container is not a video, and normalize_target would
# read a playlist URL as a watch URL with a stray query.
if [[ "$ACTION" == "items" ]]; then
    normalize_container "$TARGET"
    require_deps yt-dlp jq
    resolve_items
    exit 0
fi

normalize_target "$TARGET"

require_deps yt-dlp jq

case "$ACTION" in
info) resolve_info ;;
transcript) resolve_transcript ;;
*) resolve_stream ;;
esac
exit 0
